{#
  An additive caller fence: one NetworkPolicy selecting `app: <name>` and admitting each app in
  `from_apps` on one TCP port. It unions with the namespace baseline rather than replacing it,
  so it lists only the callers the baseline does not already admit, plus traefik where a role
  states its complete caller set on purpose (the *arr headers say why).

  The caller list is data on the service's `containers_list` entry, `netpol_from:`, so each
  caller's reason sits beside it as a YAML comment. Two places render it:

  - netpol-baseline's `networkpolicy-callers.yaml.j2` loops over every entry carrying
    `netpol_from`, gated on `netpol_baseline_enforced`.
  - A role whose deploy must carry its own fence renders it from its own template, reading
    `container_item.netpol_from`, and its entry sets `netpol_role_owned: true` so the loop
    skips it. sonarr, radarr and prowlarr do this; netpol-baseline's CLAUDE.md says when a
    policy belongs in the workload's role.

  A fence of any other shape (a namespaceSelector, an ipBlock, several ports, a caller scoped
  to a different port) stays bespoke. Do not grow this macro to absorb one.

  Arguments:
    name       Service/pod `app` label this policy selects, and the NetworkPolicy's own name.
    port       TCP port every caller is scoped to (the entry's `port`).
    from_apps  The `app` labels admitted, one `podSelector` peer each.
    enforced   False renders the allow-all body, so `netpol_baseline_enforced` stays a real
               rollback lever for the fences netpol-baseline owns. Pass it through `| bool`
               (networkpolicy-traefik.yaml.j2 has the string trap). A role-owned fence leaves
               it true: it was never gated, and the baseline's allow-all makes it moot.
#}
{% macro networkpolicy(name, port, from_apps, enforced=true) -%}
apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1
kind: NetworkPolicy
metadata:
  name: {{ name }}
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  podSelector:
    matchLabels:
      app: {{ name }}
  policyTypes:
    - Ingress
  ingress:
{% if enforced %}
    - from:
{% for app in from_apps %}
        - podSelector:
            matchLabels:
              app: {{ app }}
{% endfor %}
      ports:
        - protocol: TCP
          port: {{ port }}
{% else %}
    # OFF. One empty rule admits everything. `ingress: []` would be the exact opposite and deny
    # everything, which is the easiest thing in this file to get backwards.
    - {}
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
